name: boundary-correction
kind: inferred
shape: categorical
face: in
membrane: false
note: |
  Fires when work reaches the edge of the brief that dispatched it and finds
  the edge in the wrong place — the boundary drawn around the task admits work
  the task cannot do, or excludes work it must.

  Distinct from gap-found, which names an obstacle INSIDE a correctly-drawn
  scope. Boundary-correction says the scope line itself is misplaced, so the
  residue after doing the assigned work is not a measure of how hard the work
  was; it is a measure of how wrong the line was.

  First fired on the js-parity host-fixture wave. The brief framed the
  population as fixture-only ("a test needs ONE new file"). For 9 of one
  51-test slice that is false and unfixably so: no `.kjs` facet can supply a
  construct the JS emitter refuses to model or miscompiles. The fixture work
  was necessary and not sufficient, and the four tests still red after a
  complete port name emitter gaps, not hard fixtures.

  Value, where countable: the number of assigned units that cannot be closed
  by the assigned means.

  Detector: after finishing the assigned work completely, ask what the
  remaining failures are ABOUT. If they are systematically about a different
  artifact than the one the brief scoped, the boundary was misplaced — route
  the residue to whoever owns that artifact rather than absorbing it as
  personal shortfall.
route: surface, wake
